### Summary of behavioral additions

| Feature | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `-r` / `--recursive` | Directory operands are traversed (pre-order, sorted entries, skip symlinks, descend into subdirs, search regular files) |
| `-r` with non-directory operand | Behaves identically to without `-r` (searches the regular file) |
| Directory without `-r` | Error: "Is a directory", exit 2 |
| Prefix in default mode | Prefixed when ≥2 operands OR any operand is a directory; single regular file unprefixed |
| `-H` with `-r` | Forces prefix on all output including stdin |
| `-h` with `-r` | Suppresses all prefixes |
| Symlinks in traversal | Skipped (via `os.lstat` → `S_ISLNK` check) |
| Explicitly-named symlink operand | Followed (via `os.stat`) |
| Trailing-slash operand | No doubled separator in derived paths |
| Unreadable subdirectory during traversal | Diagnosed to stderr, traversal continues |

### Interpretation chosen

- "Ascending raw-byte order" is implemented as `sorted(entries, key=os.fsencode)`.
- "Terminate safely on deep trees" is handled by the iterative stack-based DFS (no Python recursion).
- Non-regular, non-directory, non-symlink entries (FIFOs, sockets, devices) encountered during traversal are silently skipped.
- The `use_prefix` decision for default mode is made before any I/O by stat-ing each operand (following symlinks) to detect directories.
